<Legal
  title="Privacy"
  description="What Lingua collects, what it never collects, and how to revoke consent. Telemetry is off by default."
  lastUpdated="2026-11-04"
  locale="en"
>
  <p>
    Lingua treats your code and your filesystem as private. They stay on your device unless you
    choose to send them, and telemetry and crash reports are off until you opt in. The app makes a
    few requests of its own, to check for updates and to check a license. Both are described below.
  </p>

  <h2>What we never collect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Your source code.</strong> Snippets stay on your machine. Execution history keeps run data in memory on your device, and it never leaves the device.</li>
    <li><strong>Your filesystem.</strong> Project paths, file names, and directory listings are not transmitted to us.</li>
    <li><strong>Your input.</strong> Keystrokes, clipboard contents, and command history are not logged.</li>
    <li><strong>Personal identifiers.</strong> Telemetry carries no email, name, location, or device identifier.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What we collect (only if you opt in)</h2>
  <p>
    Telemetry is <strong>off by default</strong>. The first-run consent prompt explains what gets
    sent if you opt in:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Event names from a fixed list — for example, <code>overlay.opened</code>, <code>runner.executed</code>, <code>feature.blocked</code>.</li>
    <li>The properties allowed for that event, such as a run's status or a SQL engine. They pass through a redaction layer and never include code, file paths, environment values, or license tokens.</li>
    <li>The Lingua version, the operating system, and the license tier.</li>
    <li>A random session ID that changes each time the app starts.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Events are sent to a self-hosted endpoint we control. There is no third-party analytics vendor
    in the desktop app. You can revoke consent any time from Settings → Privacy; revocation stops
    transmission immediately and any locally queued events are discarded.
  </p>

  <h2>Crash reporting</h2>
  <p>
    Crash reports are opt-in under the same consent flow, and off when no crash endpoint is
    configured. Lingua attaches no code, file paths, or project names to a report, but Electron
    includes a diagnostic minidump, and a minidump can contain data that was in memory at the crash.
  </p>

  <h2>Updates</h2>
  <p>
    The desktop app checks GitHub Releases shortly after launch and then hourly, downloads any
    update, and installs it when you quit. No setting turns this off. The web build asks
    <code>updates.linguacode.dev</code> for the latest version at startup to show an update banner.
    Both exchange version information, not your project data.
  </p>

  <h2>License and device checks</h2>
  <p>
    Paid plans use signed license tokens that are verified on your device. Lingua may also check
    them against <code>licenses.linguacode.dev</code> for activation, status refresh, device limits,
    recovery, trials, and education access. That service keeps a device identifier and coarse device
    details, such as the operating system, to enforce limits and list your devices in Settings.
    Tokens travel in authorization headers, never in URLs.
  </p>

  <h2>Code you run</h2>
  <p>
    Code you run can reach the network. Settings → Privacy → Network from running code sets Allow,
    the default, Ask or Block for the Worker, Pyodide and Ruby WASM runtimes. It is a best-effort
    guard, not a sandbox. Node, Deno, Bun, Go, Rust and system Ruby are outside it. Hosts it blocks,
    and hosts you allow when asked, are listed in Recent activity on this device with only the
    runtime and the host.
  </p>

  <h2>Clipboard</h2>
  <p>
    Apply from clipboard on panel focus is off until you turn it on, and needs Pro. With it on,
    Utilities reads the clipboard when it opens and when the window regains focus, and works out
    on your device what kind of value it holds. Only the kind stays: the text is not stored, logged
    or sent, and nothing polls the clipboard in the background.
  </p>

  <h2>AI features</h2>
  <p>
    Explain error, Explain selection, Edit with AI, Ask AI in SQL and the cron phrase composer are
    off until you set a model in Settings. Lingua ships no remote endpoint or key, and sends nothing
    until you choose Send after seeing the exact payload. Secret-looking values are redacted first.
    The payload is what the feature needs: the error, the tab's code and its runtime values only if
    you include them, the selected code or the whole file, the file with the span to edit marked,
    or the cron phrase. SQL sends table and column names and types, never rows.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Local</strong> sends only to <code>localhost</code> or <code>127.0.0.1</code>, such as Ollama. Nothing leaves the device.</li>
    <li><strong>My endpoint</strong> sends to the provider you configure, under your account and its terms.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    On desktop the key stays in the system keychain when one is available, otherwise in local
    storage. On the web it stays in the browser's local storage. Conversations are never saved, and telemetry never carries prompts, answers, endpoints or keys.
  </p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://linguacode.dev">linguacode.dev</a> is a static site on Cloudflare Pages. We do
    not run a third-party analytics script. If we add Cloudflare's first-party Web Analytics, it
    runs without cookies and without sending data to any other vendor.
  </p>

  <h2>The web build</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://app.linguacode.dev">app.linguacode.dev</a> uses the same renderer as the
    desktop app. Pyodide is served from the same origin as the app. The larger DuckDB and Ruby
    runtimes download from <code>downloads.linguacode.dev</code> when first needed.
  </p>

  <h2>The SQL engine</h2>
  <p>
    The SQL workspace runs DuckDB inside the app. Lingua ships the DuckDB extensions it uses (JSON,
    ICU and Parquet) and turns off DuckDB's download of the others from
    <code>extensions.duckdb.org</code>, so typing or running a query does not contact that host. A
    query that needs another extension fails and names it. SQL still reaches any host it names
    itself, such as a URL passed to <code>read_csv</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Database connections</h2>
  <p>
    A database connection goes from your device to the server you set up. Lingua runs no service in
    between. Turso, Neon and Cloudflare D1 go over HTTPS to those providers.
  </p>
  <p>
    On desktop, passwords, tokens and key passphrases are encrypted with your system keychain, or
    stay in memory until you quit for a connection that asks each session, and always where no
    keychain is available. They are never written in plain text or logged, and the interface cannot
    read a saved one back. The list of saved connections has no secrets. It holds names, hosts, users
    and databases in a file only you can read on macOS and Linux. In the browser, Turso and Neon
    connections last for the tab and their tokens stay in memory.
  </p>
  <p>
    Results and driver errors from a connection stay in the session and leave run capsules. A query
    on a connection reaches disk with its secret literals masked. Telemetry carries the engine and
    the step that failed, never a host, user, query or row. A crash report's minidump can hold what
    was in memory, such as a password in use or rows, which is one reason crash reports are opt-in.
  </p>
  <p>
    For an SSH tunnel, Lingua reads <code>~/.ssh/known_hosts</code> and <code>~/.ssh/config</code>
    and does not write them. It opens a private key from <code>~/.ssh</code> only when you pick it,
    your ssh config names it, or you save a connection imported with it. A saved connection keeps
    the key's path, not its content. Host keys you trust are kept in a file of Lingua's own, and
    with the SSH agent the agent holds the key.
  </p>
  <p>
    Importing connections reads the file you pick, the file beside it that completes it, or the
    <code>.env</code> files at the open project's root, and drops every password. An export has no secrets.
  </p>

